Usage: find [path...] [expression]

Search for files in a directory hierarchy.
If no path is given, '.' is used.

OPTIONS:
  -name ARG               base of file name (the path with the leading
                          directories removed) matches shell pattern PATTERN
  -iname ARG              like -name, but the match is case insensitive
  -path ARG               file name matches shell pattern PATTERN
  -ipath ARG              like -path, but the match is case insensitive
  -wholename ARG          same as -path
  -iwholename ARG         same as -ipath, but case insensitive
  -type ARG               file is of type c: b,d,p,f,l,s,D [only d,f,l,p are
                          supported]
  -xtype ARG              like -type but checks the opposite symlink resolution
                          state [only d,f,l,p are supported]
  -execdir ARG            execute command from the subdirectory containing the
                          matched file
  -D, --debug ARG         print find traversal diagnostics [DIFFERS: Windows
                          diagnostics]
  -nowarn                 turn off file traversal warnings
  -warn                   turn on file traversal warnings
  -ignore_readdir_race    ignore errors when entries disappear during traversal
  -noignore_readdir_race  report errors when entries disappear during traversal
  -nouser                 file has no user corresponding to its owner ID
  -used ARG               file was last accessed n days after its status was
                          last changed
  -perm ARG               file's permission bits match MODE [PARTIAL: octal
                          modes only]
  -readable               file can be read
  -writable               file can be written
  -executable             file can be executed or searched
  -inum ARG               file has inode/file-index number N
  -links ARG              file has N hard links
  -user ARG               file is owned by user name or UID
  -group ARG              file belongs to group name or GID
  -nogroup                file has no group corresponding to its group ID
                          [DIFFERS: Windows account mapping]
  -uid ARG                file owner's numeric UID matches N
  -gid ARG                file group's numeric GID matches N
  -size ARG               file uses n units of space
  -empty                  file is empty and is either a regular file or a
                          directory
  -amin ARG               file was last accessed n minutes ago
  -atime ARG              file was last accessed n*24 hours ago
  -cmin ARG               file status was last changed n minutes ago
  -ctime ARG              file status was last changed n*24 hours ago
  -mtime ARG              file data was last modified n*24 hours ago
  -mmin ARG               file data was last modified n minutes ago
  -mindepth NUM           descend at least LEVELS levels of directories before
                          tests
  -maxdepth NUM           descend at most LEVELS levels of directories below
                          starting-points
  -print                  print the full file name on the standard output
  -print0                 print the full file name on the standard output,
                          followed by a null character
  -fprint ARG             print the full file name into FILE
  -fprint0 ARG            print the full file name into FILE, followed by a null
                          character
  -L                      follow symbolic links
  -H                      do not follow symbolic links, except while processing
                          command line arguments
  -P                      never follow symbolic links (default)
  -follow                 dereference symbolic links
  -O NUM                  set optimization level (0-3, currently ignored)
  -delete                 delete files
  -exec ARG               execute command
  -ok ARG                 execute command after confirmation
  -okdir ARG              execute command from the subdirectory after
                          confirmation [DIFFERS: Windows command prompt]
  -ls                     list current file in ls -dils format [DIFFERS: Windows
                          metadata]
  -fls ARG                list current file in ls -dils format into FILE
                          [DIFFERS: Windows metadata]
  -printf ARG             print format [PARTIAL:
                          %p,%f,%h,%l,%H,%P,%y,%Y,%s,%b,%k,%d,%D,%F,%g,%G,%o,%u,
                          %U,%S,%m,%M,%n,%i,%A@,%B@,%C@,%T@,%AY,%Am,%Ad,%AH,%AM,
                          %AS,%Aj,%BY,%Bm,%Bd,%BH,%BM,%BS,%Bj,%CY,%Cm,%Cd,%CH,%C
                          M,%CS,%Cj,%TY,%Tm,%Td,%TH,%TM,%TS,%Tj,%%]
  -fprintf ARG            print format into FILE
  -prune                  prune tree
  -quit                   exit immediately
  -true                   always true
  -false                  always false
  -regex ARG              whole path matches regular expression
  -iregex ARG             whole path matches regular expression, case
                          insensitive
  -newer ARG              file was modified more recently than the reference
                          file
  -anewer ARG             file was accessed more recently than the reference
                          file
  -cnewer ARG             file status changed more recently than the reference
                          file [DIFFERS: Windows change time]
  -fstype ARG             file is on a file system of type TYPE [DIFFERS:
                          Windows volume file system name]
  -ilname ARG             symbolic link target matches shell pattern PATTERN,
                          case insensitive
  -lname ARG              symbolic link target matches shell pattern PATTERN
  -neweraa ARG            compare access time to reference access time
  -neweraB ARG            compare access time to reference birth time
  -newerac ARG            compare access time to reference change time
  -neweram ARG            compare access time to reference modify time
  -newerat ARG            compare access time to literal time
  -newerBa ARG            compare birth time to reference access time
  -newerBB ARG            compare birth time to reference birth time
  -newerBc ARG            compare birth time to reference change time
  -newerBm ARG            compare birth time to reference modify time
  -newerBt ARG            compare birth time to literal time
  -newerca ARG            compare change time to reference access time
  -newercB ARG            compare change time to reference birth time
  -newercc ARG            compare change time to reference change time
  -newercm ARG            compare change time to reference modify time
  -newerct ARG            compare change time to literal time
  -newerma ARG            compare modify time to reference access time
  -newermB ARG            compare modify time to reference birth time
  -newermc ARG            compare modify time to reference change time
  -newermm ARG            compare modify time to reference modify time
  -newermt ARG            compare modify time to literal time
  -samefile ARG           file refers to the same file as the reference file
  -files0-from ARG        read starting points from NUL-delimited file
  -depth                  process each directory's contents before the directory
                          itself
  -d                      same as -depth
  -daystart               measure times from start of today
  -mount                  do not descend into other file systems
  -xdev                   same as -mount
  -noleaf                 do not optimize by assuming 2+ hard links
  -regextype ARG          set regex syntax for later -regex/-iregex
  !                       negate expression
  -not                    negate expression
  -a                      and expression
  -and                    and expression
  -o                      or expression
  -or                     or expression
  -help                   display this help and exit
  -version                output version information and exit
  -context ARG            file matches SELinux security context CTX
      --help              display this help and exit
  -V, --version           output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
